Ask this system for its descriptor limit, so fdwalk has a range to walk

getrlimit_NOFILE_max() is written as a list of systems whose getrlimit() is
known to be async-signal-safe -- Linux, glibc, the BSDs, Apple -- and returns
RLIM_INFINITY for anything not on the list.  fdwalk() then treats infinity as
"cannot close infinitely many descriptors" and gives up:

        errno = ENFILE;
        return -1;

which surfaces, several layers up, as

        Failed to execute child process "bash":
        Failed to fdwalk: Too many open files in system

Nothing is out of files.  ENFILE is a stand-in for "I do not know how many
descriptors there could be", and every attempt to start a shell fails with it
-- so the terminal opens, immediately puts up that dialog, and closes.

LikeOS is added to the arm that simply asks getrlimit(), for the reason that
arm exists: this libc's getrlimit is a thin wrapper around a single syscall
that takes no lock and allocates nothing, so calling it between fork and exec
is safe.  It reports RLIMIT_NOFILE as OPEN_MAX -- the real, bounded size of
the descriptor table -- so the walk terminates.

--- a/src/missing.cc
+++ b/src/missing.cc
@@ -107,21 +107,23 @@
 }
 #endif /* __linux__ */
 
-#ifdef __GLIBC__
+#if defined(__GLIBC__) || defined(__LIKEOS__)
 {
         struct rlimit rlim;
 
         /* Use getrlimit() function provided by the system if it is known to be
          * async-signal safe.
          *
-         * According to the glibc manual, getrlimit is AS-safe.
+         * According to the glibc manual, getrlimit is AS-safe.  On LikeOS it
+         * is a wrapper around one syscall that takes no lock and allocates
+         * nothing, which is the property being relied on here.
          */
         if (getrlimit(RLIMIT_NOFILE, &rlim) == 0)
                 return rlim.rlim_max;
 }
 
         /* fallback */
-#endif /* __GLIBC__ */
+#endif /* __GLIBC__ || __LIKEOS__ */
 
 #endif /* HAVE_SYS_RESOURCE_H */
 
